# kernel-ia64.img -- Linux 6.6.23 for the ia64 "virt" machine (Itanium 2, UP)
#
# Runs on qemu-system-ia64 -M virt from the qemu fork (lordrasmus/qemu,
# branch uclibc-ng); upstream qemu never had system emulation for ia64.
# The image is the stripped vmlinux (ELF), loaded at its physical
# addresses.
#
#   qemu-system-ia64 -M virt -nographic -m 1G -kernel kernel-ia64.img \
#     -initrd rootfs.img.xz -append console=ttyS0
#
# There is no firmware image: the machine answers the EFI, SAL and PAL
# calls itself and builds the ACPI tables (MADT with one LSAPIC and the
# I/O SAPIC, a DSDT announcing COM1 as PNP0501), so the kernel finds its
# console through PNPACPI and SERIAL_8250_PNP.
#
# 6.6 is the last line with ia64 (removed in v6.7).
#
# Patches, each one fix, against linux-6.6.23:
#   kernel-ia64-up-set_pte.patch  the !SMP branches of
#                                 ptep_test_and_clear_young and
#                                 ptep_set_wrprotect call set_pte_at
#                                 before it is defined; gcc >= 14 makes
#                                 that an error (and the link fails)
#
# Build: kernel-ia64.config is tiger_defconfig without SMP and modules,
# MCKINLEY, 16 KB pages, FLATMEM, PNPACPI, SERIAL_8250_PNP, DEVTMPFS
# (_MOUNT), RD_XZ, PRINTK_TIME, no SCSI/ATA/USB/network drivers.
#
#   cd linux-6.6.23
#   patch -p1 < kernel-ia64-up-set_pte.patch
#   cp kernel-ia64.config .config
#   make ARCH=ia64 CROSS_COMPILE=ia64-openadk-linux-uclibc- olddefconfig
#   make ARCH=ia64 CROSS_COMPILE=ia64-openadk-linux-uclibc- \
#     KCFLAGS="-Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration \
#              -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types -Wno-error=int-conversion" \
#     vmlinux
#   ia64-openadk-linux-uclibc-strip -o kernel-ia64.img vmlinux
#
# The KCFLAGS are for gcc 15, which turned these warnings into errors
# after ia64 was gone.  The toolchain is the CI's
# toolchain-ia64-gcc-15.3.0-lt-static.
#
# Known: an SMP kernel needs qemu with 7716ae961f or later (TCs dropped
# on firmware calls); this UP image does not.
